WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.125 --> 00:00:02.708
(steady music)

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:05.580
- What's up, friends?

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:08.520
Michael here to reminisce
about a simpler time.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.020
The year was 2013.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:12.690
My cholesterol was simply high for my age.

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:14.220
I still necessitated haircuts,

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:16.530
and there was a new
serialized political thriller

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:18.660
starring an Academy Award-winning actor

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:21.157
whom we had no reason to
suspect was a sex offender.

00:00:21.157 --> 00:00:24.180
"House of Cards" hit
Netflix to instant acclaim

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:25.770
and a million network executives

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:27.300
had simultaneous panic attacks

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.100
because the television landscape had

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:30.930
apparently changed forever.

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:32.400
Flash forward a decade,

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:33.450
and it's fair to conclude

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:35.940
the revolution will not
be Netflix and chilled

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:38.600
or Netflixed and chilled.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:39.433
Is that it?

00:00:39.433 --> 00:00:41.130
I don't know. That means
to have sex, right?

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:42.480
That's because, these days,

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.640
streaming services are going through it.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.640
In June, Netflix fired 300 employees

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:50.100
as the company lost 70% of its value.

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.260
Then Warner Brothers announced the HBO Max

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:53.520
and Discovery+ merger,

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.430
sending the internet into
a collective freak out.

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:56.430
In the weeks since,

00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:58.170
HBO has, among other things,

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:00.607
shelved the already filmed
$90 million feature film

00:01:00.607 --> 00:01:02.880
"Batgirl," weathered
rumors of looming layoffs,

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:06.120
and purged its archives
of many beloved TV shows.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.040
Hard to believe that just a few years ago

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:10.170
media forecasters confidently predicted

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:12.780
that streaming would kill
television as we knew it.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:14.910
But with companies now firing employees,

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:16.380
consolidating their platforms,

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.690
and canceling much anticipated releases,

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:21.210
we've gotta ask, is
streaming on the way out?

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:23.220
Or has it turned the media landscape

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:26.190
into a content machine
that's too big to fail?

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:28.290
Let's find out in this "Wisecrack Edition,

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:30.390
Did Streaming Content Kill Culture?"

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:33.330
Before we can reflect on
content, culture and streaming,

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:35.850
it's worth looking to the
entertainment industry's past.

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:37.470
As reporter Brooks Barnes notes,

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:40.020
Hollywood's no stranger to titanic moments

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:41.407
of instant change, writing,

00:01:41.407 --> 00:01:44.910
"Once a generation, Hollywood
experiences a seismic shift."

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:46.830
In these moments, all accepted facts

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:48.750
about people's entertainment
consumption are called

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:51.420
into question, and premature
eulogies are written

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:53.670
for once-beloved forms of mass media.

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:55.560
For example, when it began broadcasting

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:59.040
in the early 1950s, TV
started cannibalizing content

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:01.980
from the then reigning mass
entertainment medium, radio.

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:04.440
Popular shows like the
Western drama "Gunsmoke"

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:06.900
and the family sitcom "Father
Knows Best" were adapted

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:07.740
for the screen.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:09.780
While some of these shows
kept a foot in radio,

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.210
television would eventually
render narrative radio

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:13.800
essentially obsolete,

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.870
though if you've ever
listened to a podcast,

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:19.230
you know audio-only entertainment
has reemerged as a force

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:21.240
and you probably also know discount codes

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:24.480
for mattresses, online gambling
and fancy toothbrushes,

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:27.210
but network television
would barely get its moment

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:29.700
in the sun before mainstream
media started predicting

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:30.810
its own swan song.

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:34.680
Sick of your stodgy NBC,
ABC and CBS offerings?

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:38.856
Well, circa August 1st, 1981,
you were in for a real trip.

00:02:38.856 --> 00:02:42.180
♪ Video killed the radio star ♪

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:44.610
- [Michael] MTV is remembered
for popularizing the art form

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:46.080
of music videos, but importantly,

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:47.580
it also helped drive consumers

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:49.290
to cable television subscriptions.

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:51.350
- I want my MTV!

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:54.960
- By 1989, an estimated 60%
of U.S. households had cable.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.540
Once seen as the minor
leagues of television

00:02:57.540 --> 00:02:59.820
where reruns and old
movies reigned supreme,

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:01.800
cable suddenly became a real threat

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.420
to network television's dominance.

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:06.060
And by the late '90s, it was
cutting into its profits.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:08.730
What's more, reality TV's meteoric rise

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:10.950
in the form of "American
Idol" and "Survivor,"

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:12.660
coupled with the 2008 writer strike,

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:15.480
suggested that network
television was on the outs,

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:18.420
and "Mad Men's" Emmy win
marked the first time

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:20.730
a basic cable television show took home

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:21.840
the prestigious award,

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:24.210
but cable couldn't stay on top forever,

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:26.280
and more change predictably came.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:28.350
Although many have called the early 2010s

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:30.457
the golden age of cable, by 2013,

00:03:30.457 --> 00:03:32.310
"The Economist" was already asking,

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:34.440
is Netflix killing cable television?

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:37.110
Rising to prominence
as a DVD mailer service

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:39.870
that attempted to replace
the truly irreplaceable,

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:42.330
Netflix quickly pivoted
into video streaming

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:43.440
and original content.

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:45.180
As "Variety" announced in 2020,

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:48.000
cable TV's gravy train went off the rails

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:49.110
when streaming arrived.

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:50.310
Hollywood became terrified

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:52.380
about Netflix's rising cultural dominance

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:54.780
and serious film ambassador,
Steven Spielberg,

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:57.660
campaigned against even
considering Netflix films

00:03:57.660 --> 00:03:59.490
for top Academy Awards honors.

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:02.370
- Once you commit to a television format,

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:03.840
you're a TV movie.

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:07.680
You certainly, if it's a
good show, deserve an Emmy,

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:08.640
but not on Oscar.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:10.800
- Given how TV reporters, economists,

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:11.670
and even the creator

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:14.310
of "E.T." himself has propped Netflix up

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:15.990
to be a big cultural boogieman,

00:04:15.990 --> 00:04:19.260
you'd think the company
would be too big to fail,

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:21.030
but with the recent loss of subscribers,

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:23.430
the decrease in market value
and the string of layoffs,

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:24.300
it's tempting to buy

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:27.270
into the media's preemptive
obituary for streaming,

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:28.650
arguably though, Netflix

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:30.660
and other streaming
platforms have hit a wall

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:31.680
of their own making.

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:32.610
And as some of argued,

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:35.460
they simply have no more room for growth.

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:37.087
As journalist Louis Chilton writes,

00:04:37.087 --> 00:04:39.750
"Financially, streaming
has always been coasting by

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:40.830
on vibes alone.

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:42.420
You can't build an entire business

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:44.340
around exponential subscriber growth.

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:46.320
Eventually, you're simply going to run out

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:47.250
of new customers."

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:49.260
Rather than eking out hundreds of millions

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:50.550
of dollars in theater tickets,

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:52.860
the streaming model
means releasing content

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:55.380
enticing enough to keep
current subscribers paying

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:56.820
for the privilege of watching it,

00:04:56.820 --> 00:04:59.490
and ideally, convincing
new clients to sign on too,

00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:02.220
even as the pool of potential
recruits keeps dwindling.

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:04.200
And how do you convince
people to keep mailing

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:06.510
in 999 pennies a month?

00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:08.250
By the logic of streaming services,

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:10.590
you start producing stuff to watch,

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:12.750
lots of stuff to watch.

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:14.550
Netflix started growing its empire

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:16.860
with a budget in the
humble hundreds of millions

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:20.850
that quickly ballooned
into $17 billion by 2021.

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:22.740
I wonder what $17 billion would do

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:25.230
to the increasing amount
of student loan debt, huh?

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:26.063
Joseph Biden?

00:05:26.063 --> 00:05:27.450
And the mass proliferation

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:30.930
of stuff to watch rendered
streaming's rise inextricable

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:33.420
from the overall emergence
of the content industry.

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:36.150
The content industry
basically includes any company

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:37.650
that produces digital content,

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:40.170
i.e. stuff that you stare at on your phone

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:41.460
when you could be, I don't know,

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:43.050
like connecting with humans or something.

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:44.400
While rumblings about the rise

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:46.200
of the industry started in the '90s,

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:48.360
It wasn't until the
emergence of search engines,

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:50.520
streaming platforms, and
faster internet speed

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:51.540
that it really exploded.

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:53.107
As scholar Kate Eichhorn writes,

00:05:53.107 --> 00:05:55.980
"Content sometimes conveys information,

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:57.870
tells a story or entertains,

00:05:57.870 --> 00:05:59.400
but it doesn't need to do any

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:01.680
of these things to circulate effectively."

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:02.520
Viewed in this way,

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:05.550
the primary purpose for
content's existence is,

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:08.160
according to economist
Bharat Anand, "Circulation."

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:09.870
And that's what distinguishes content

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:11.820
from previous forms of entertainment.

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:13.560
Eichhorn notes that the material

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:16.440
and aesthetic aspects of the
content lose significance.

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:18.060
She writes, "In the world of content,

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:21.630
genre, medium and form
become secondary concerns.

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:24.300
And in some instances, seem
to disappear entirely."

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:26.670
This is epitomized by
the streaming ecosystem.

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:29.310
As Annika Brawl Martins
writes, "The cultural practice

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:32.490
of consuming video on Netflix
doesn't necessarily translate

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:36.360
into experiencing it as
television, but rather as content."

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:37.500
We can see some of this

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:40.590
in the way that Netflix has
eroded conventional differences

00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:42.240
between film and television,

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:44.040
especially with the
rise of binge watching.

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:45.697
As scholar Anne Friedberg argues,

00:06:45.697 --> 00:06:48.060
"The differences between
the media of movies,

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:50.730
television and computers
are rapidly diminishing."

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:53.130
Form becomes increasingly secondary

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:55.740
to content's ability to
attract the most eyeballs.

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:57.540
And of course, then to use those eyeballs

00:06:57.540 --> 00:06:58.950
to collect delicious data.

00:06:58.950 --> 00:07:02.100
In this way, a piece of
content's success can be judged,

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:04.080
not by say, it's message,

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:05.700
if there is indeed a message,

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:08.640
it's defined explicitly
by how widely it spreads.

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:10.560
Consumption is the entire point,

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:12.030
not the happy side effect.

00:07:12.030 --> 00:07:13.260
It's kind of like a Big Mac, right?

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:16.110
The point isn't that it's
a high quality burger,

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:20.100
but that it's a burger that's
consumed around the world 24/7

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:21.720
because of this, Eichhorn argues

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:24.900
that content is the "Ultimate
expression of neoliberalism

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:26.550
and that it represents a shift away

00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:28.470
from understanding cultural production

00:07:28.470 --> 00:07:30.930
in relation to aesthetic
and material categories

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:33.540
and towards understanding
cultural production solely

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:35.460
through a market-focused lens."

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:37.980
That is to say, in the
new content landscape,

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:40.440
pieces of culture only matter insofar

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:41.670
as they bring in profit.

00:07:41.670 --> 00:07:44.550
If beauty or meaning is
the mark of quality art

00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:46.890
and enjoyment is the mark
of quality entertainment,

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:49.620
bankability is the mark
of quality content.

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:51.810
This state of affairs feels eerily

00:07:51.810 --> 00:07:54.390
in line with the mid-20th
century assessment

00:07:54.390 --> 00:07:57.690
of mass entertainment made
by scholar Theodore Adorno.

00:07:57.690 --> 00:07:58.523
In recent decades,

00:07:58.523 --> 00:08:01.080
he's been fairly
criticized for being a bit

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:03.540
of a curmudgeon for his
critique of the mass culture

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:06.780
and especially of classic
Hollywood films and jazz music.

00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:08.880
To be clear, both of those things rip,

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:10.650
but when it comes to
modern streaming series

00:08:10.650 --> 00:08:11.940
like "FBoy Island,"

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:13.860
we think his argument bears consideration.

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:15.767
Along with philosopher Max Horkheimer,

00:08:15.767 --> 00:08:18.420
Adorno conceived of
mid-century entertainment,

00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:20.160
what they called the culture industry,

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:21.570
as a sort of assembly line

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:23.400
where art and entertainment are reduced

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:26.370
to mere products created
solely to produce profit.

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:28.680
As they write "The
cultural entities typical

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:31.680
of the culture industry are
no longer also commodities.

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:33.990
They are commodities through and through."

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:34.823
In this way,

00:08:34.823 --> 00:08:36.540
they thought art started being valued

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:39.270
for its ability to sell
rather than its ability

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:41.640
to impart a message, elicit emotions,

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:43.410
or express an aesthetic vision.

00:08:43.410 --> 00:08:45.930
An essential part of this
assembly line logic is

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:47.730
that the entertainment industry recycles

00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:50.820
the same storytelling
conventions ad nauseam

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:52.890
until the distinctions
between individual works

00:08:52.890 --> 00:08:55.260
of art become entirely meaningless.

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:57.300
And again, while we
don't think this applies

00:08:57.300 --> 00:09:00.270
to say, the improvisational
jazz of John Coltrane,

00:09:00.270 --> 00:09:02.550
we do think it's a fitting encapsulation

00:09:02.550 --> 00:09:04.530
of the streaming industrial complex

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:06.870
where there are seemingly
infinite reality shows

00:09:06.870 --> 00:09:09.840
about rich people who love
fashion and live for drama

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:12.510
or cute couples who help
people build their dream homes.

00:09:12.510 --> 00:09:14.760
With an estimated 1,600 shows

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:17.220
and several hundred movies
made and distributed

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:19.170
on various stringing platforms each year,

00:09:19.170 --> 00:09:21.300
there's never been more
content to consume.

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:22.740
And if you're listening,
streaming networks,

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:26.190
why not make it 1,601 and give us a shot?

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:27.600
But the assembly line logic

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:29.400
of it all becomes glaringly apparent

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:30.780
when you consider how a platform

00:09:30.780 --> 00:09:32.940
like Netflix generates its ideas.

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:34.080
- Netflix, you're green lit.

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:34.950
Who am I speaking with?

00:09:34.950 --> 00:09:37.200
- Specifically, the
platform relies heavily

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:40.170
on its $1 billion artificial
intelligence program

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:41.760
to guide its content selection.

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:42.780
If I was running things,

00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:44.160
after this clip, then it would cut

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:47.790
to the Dr. Evil from "Austin
Powers" saying, "$1 billion,"

00:09:47.790 --> 00:09:49.110
or doesn't say "1 million?"

00:09:49.110 --> 00:09:50.310
See, I (beep) it up. Nevermind.

00:09:50.310 --> 00:09:51.750
For example, "House of Cards,"

00:09:51.750 --> 00:09:53.520
a remake of a BBC program,

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:55.290
was algorithmically generated

00:09:55.290 --> 00:09:57.360
to attract the Venn diagram intersection

00:09:57.360 --> 00:09:59.490
of Kevin Spacey fans
who liked David Fincher

00:09:59.490 --> 00:10:01.200
and enjoyed British political thrillers.

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:02.490
Here, we see how a work

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:05.190
of entertainment is reduced
to the sum of its parts

00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:07.320
rather than, say, it's artistic integrity.

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:09.480
It's like when KFC had
the Double Down Sandwich

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:11.070
where the bread was fried chicken

00:10:11.070 --> 00:10:12.540
and where the chicken would normally go

00:10:12.540 --> 00:10:13.530
was bacon and cheese.

00:10:13.530 --> 00:10:14.940
And literally just talking about it,

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:16.830
I feel my LDL just spiking.

00:10:16.830 --> 00:10:18.360
We talk about this in our video

00:10:18.360 --> 00:10:20.820
about why all Netflix films look the same

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:21.720
but it bears repeating.

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:24.600
The result of this AI
content selection has led

00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:27.390
to the proliferation of a
sort of mutant film created

00:10:27.390 --> 00:10:30.420
to tick as many box office
happy criteria as possible.

00:10:30.420 --> 00:10:31.980
This is also why we get a lot

00:10:31.980 --> 00:10:34.500
of content based on existing
intellectual property,

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:37.140
whether it's the million
Marvel shows on Disney+,

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:38.880
or "Bridgerton," which
is based on a series

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:39.810
of romance novels,

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:42.630
or how about the show based on
a "New York Magazine" article

00:10:42.630 --> 00:10:44.550
about Anna Delvey, or "The Staircase,"

00:10:44.550 --> 00:10:46.740
which is based on a documentary series,

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:48.900
which is based on a real life true crime.

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:49.920
In case you missed that one,

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:52.440
it's a narrative show based
on a documentary series

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:55.950
about a real life murder
that an owl did to a lady.

00:10:55.950 --> 00:10:58.590
I am a proud member of Owl Theory Nation.

00:10:58.590 --> 00:11:00.210
Shout it out in the
comments if you are too.

00:11:00.210 --> 00:11:01.410
The assembly line logic

00:11:01.410 --> 00:11:04.470
of streaming also explains
the rise in reality TV shows

00:11:04.470 --> 00:11:07.260
which are significantly
less expensive to produce

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:08.820
than conventional narrative content

00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:11.460
while also allowing for
other money making avenues

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:13.110
like seamless product placement.

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:15.540
Take HBO, famously the capital H

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:16.920
Home of prestige television.

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:19.890
Its merger with Discovery and
with it acquisition of reams

00:11:19.890 --> 00:11:21.780
of reality television is indicative

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:23.940
of just how much streaming has changed

00:11:23.940 --> 00:11:26.040
from its days of high quality programming

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:28.890
to its increased reliance
on low budget reality shows

00:11:28.890 --> 00:11:31.170
that are conducive to endless spinoffs,

00:11:31.170 --> 00:11:33.217
like "FBoy Gaza Strip,"

00:11:33.217 --> 00:11:37.117
"FBoy Emergency Room,"
"FBoy Alaskan Mortuary,"

00:11:37.117 --> 00:11:40.140
"FBoy Cruise Ship Stranded
at Sea With a Disease on It,"

00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:42.210
or "FBoy Pediatrician's Office."

00:11:42.210 --> 00:11:44.280
No wonder that streaming has coincided

00:11:44.280 --> 00:11:46.770
with the slow devaluation of mass culture

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:47.820
and the people who make it.

00:11:47.820 --> 00:11:50.070
Streaming services have
used their classification

00:11:50.070 --> 00:11:53.820
as new media to deny
entertainment workers fair pay,

00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:55.860
especially those doing thankless jobs

00:11:55.860 --> 00:11:57.150
on film and TV sets.

00:11:57.150 --> 00:11:59.730
Residuals, once a vital source of income

00:11:59.730 --> 00:12:01.500
for entertainment workers,
have been slashed.

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:04.170
Meaning, workers ranging
from production designers

00:12:04.170 --> 00:12:07.350
to lead actors are often
paid only once for their work

00:12:07.350 --> 00:12:10.320
or compensated significantly less overall

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:13.860
because they can't rely on
reruns, DVD sales, et cetera.

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:15.720
Clearly, the streaming
industry's assembly line

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:18.930
of content encapsulates the
ethos of the content industry,

00:12:18.930 --> 00:12:20.250
and with it, the acceleration

00:12:20.250 --> 00:12:21.960
of Adorno and Horkheimer's logic

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:24.300
about the culture industry's
cheapening of art.

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:26.820
Fittingly then, streaming's
dominance also seems

00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:28.650
to be shifting the way we view content,

00:12:28.650 --> 00:12:30.480
both literally and conceptually.

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:32.280
While television fans were formerly

00:12:32.280 --> 00:12:35.160
at the mercy of weekly air
times to catch "Seinfeld,"

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:37.350
- A car just bashed into a parked car

00:12:37.350 --> 00:12:39.333
and sped off right on my block.

00:12:40.230 --> 00:12:41.640
- You gotta follow that car.

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:42.900
- [Jerry] What?
(audience laughing)

00:12:42.900 --> 00:12:45.210
- Streaming services have
enabled the consumption

00:12:45.210 --> 00:12:47.160
of an entire show all at once.

00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:50.220
As Netflix's CEO put it,
"We're competing with sleep."

00:12:50.220 --> 00:12:52.920
The very format of the
platforms is conducive to this.

00:12:52.920 --> 00:12:56.100
Netflix's auto play function
facilitates endless watching,

00:12:56.100 --> 00:12:57.870
essentially choosing for the viewer

00:12:57.870 --> 00:13:00.810
to continue the next leg
of their viewing binge.

00:13:00.810 --> 00:13:02.130
It's no wonder then that much

00:13:02.130 --> 00:13:05.010
of our entertainment consumption
is done absentmindedly

00:13:05.010 --> 00:13:06.600
with the exception of video games,

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:08.850
which remain arguably the most active form

00:13:08.850 --> 00:13:09.840
of cultural consumption,

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:11.640
so gamers, keep on fighting

00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:13.050
against the dying of the light.

00:13:13.050 --> 00:13:14.617
As writer Jake Pitre argues,

00:13:14.617 --> 00:13:15.630
"In a binge culture,

00:13:15.630 --> 00:13:18.930
the banal pleasures of
zoning out coexist with

00:13:18.930 --> 00:13:20.910
and play an increasingly strategic role

00:13:20.910 --> 00:13:23.100
in maintaining the intensive pleasures

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:24.240
of switched-on viewing."

00:13:24.240 --> 00:13:25.230
The rising phenomenon

00:13:25.230 --> 00:13:28.050
of second screens compounds
the zoning out effect,

00:13:28.050 --> 00:13:29.730
with viewers dividing their attention

00:13:29.730 --> 00:13:31.020
between multiple stimuli.

00:13:31.020 --> 00:13:32.310
Also, if you have a boring job

00:13:32.310 --> 00:13:33.420
and you need multiple stimuli,

00:13:33.420 --> 00:13:35.310
on Thursdays at 11:00 AM Pacific,

00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:37.020
check out our stream "Wisecrack Live."

00:13:37.020 --> 00:13:38.340
You can have it on with headphones

00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:39.570
and you like act like you're working

00:13:39.570 --> 00:13:42.450
but really we're just
hanging out, watching videos.

00:13:42.450 --> 00:13:44.010
I'll probably get
unhinged for a little bit.

00:13:44.010 --> 00:13:45.090
It'll be fun. Check it out.

00:13:45.090 --> 00:13:46.317
Arguably, this is why much

00:13:46.317 --> 00:13:49.680
of the streaming services' low
impact content works so well.

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:51.780
It doesn't demand rigorous attention

00:13:51.780 --> 00:13:53.400
but it does demand our time,

00:13:53.400 --> 00:13:55.530
lots and lots of our time.

00:13:55.530 --> 00:13:58.320
I guess it takes time
to spot all of the Fboys

00:13:58.320 --> 00:13:59.153
on the island.

00:13:59.153 --> 00:14:00.360
It's like, where's Waldo?

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:01.470
Except Waldo (beep).

00:14:01.470 --> 00:14:02.640
And that's the real reason

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:04.200
streaming services aren't going anywhere,

00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:07.080
because they have a loyal army
of workers supporting them.

00:14:07.080 --> 00:14:08.130
Namely, you.

00:14:08.130 --> 00:14:09.840
See, according to Adorno and Horkheimer,

00:14:09.840 --> 00:14:11.940
the assembly line nature
of cultural production

00:14:11.940 --> 00:14:12.930
under late capitalism

00:14:12.930 --> 00:14:15.120
and the repetition of
entertainment's narratives

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:17.700
and forms essentially turns
consuming entertainment

00:14:17.700 --> 00:14:19.020
into mechanized work.

00:14:19.020 --> 00:14:21.090
They write, "Amusement
under late capitalism

00:14:21.090 --> 00:14:22.620
is the prolongation of work.

00:14:22.620 --> 00:14:24.210
It is sought after as an escape

00:14:24.210 --> 00:14:25.740
from the mechanized work process

00:14:25.740 --> 00:14:26.670
and to recruit strength

00:14:26.670 --> 00:14:28.530
in order to be able to cope with it again.

00:14:28.530 --> 00:14:31.320
But at the same time, a
mechanization has such power

00:14:31.320 --> 00:14:32.850
over a man's leisure and happiness.

00:14:32.850 --> 00:14:34.860
And so, it profoundly
determines the manufacturer

00:14:34.860 --> 00:14:37.560
of amusement goods that his
experiences are inevitably

00:14:37.560 --> 00:14:40.020
after-images of the work process itself.

00:14:40.020 --> 00:14:41.790
What happens at work in the factory

00:14:41.790 --> 00:14:43.770
or in the office can only be escaped

00:14:43.770 --> 00:14:46.140
by approximation to it
in one's leisure time."

00:14:46.140 --> 00:14:48.750
In this way, while we may see a long night

00:14:48.750 --> 00:14:51.210
of streaming as an escape
from the humdrum repetition

00:14:51.210 --> 00:14:53.370
of our day jobs, consuming
it becomes something

00:14:53.370 --> 00:14:55.620
of a part-time job in and of itself.

00:14:55.620 --> 00:14:57.270
But hey, what else would
we do at that time? Right?

00:14:57.270 --> 00:14:59.370
We're gonna like hang out
and reflect on our lives

00:14:59.370 --> 00:15:00.600
and maybe reflect on our lives

00:15:00.600 --> 00:15:02.490
in relation to society
and our place in society.

00:15:02.490 --> 00:15:04.650
Maybe we'd gather with
our friends and neighbors,

00:15:04.650 --> 00:15:07.320
reflect collectively,
organize to change a society

00:15:07.320 --> 00:15:08.563
that's grinding us all into dust.

00:15:08.563 --> 00:15:09.420
(Michael laughs)
(wind whipping)

00:15:09.420 --> 00:15:11.040
Yeah, that'd be, that'd be random.

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:13.770
Importantly, Adorno and
Horkheimer also argue

00:15:13.770 --> 00:15:17.070
that the culture industry
has significant ramifications

00:15:17.070 --> 00:15:18.780
on our political agency.

00:15:18.780 --> 00:15:20.730
Because it's produced and consumed

00:15:20.730 --> 00:15:22.980
under the logic of profit
minded neoliberalism,

00:15:22.980 --> 00:15:24.390
products of the culture industry

00:15:24.390 --> 00:15:26.280
typically uphold dominant social

00:15:26.280 --> 00:15:27.330
and political values.

00:15:27.330 --> 00:15:29.737
As scholar Kumars Salehi writes for Verso,

00:15:29.737 --> 00:15:31.890
"Chief among Adorno's concerns is that,

00:15:31.890 --> 00:15:33.090
in the culture industry,

00:15:33.090 --> 00:15:35.070
what gets left out is an understanding

00:15:35.070 --> 00:15:38.400
not only of the real
mechanisms of power in society

00:15:38.400 --> 00:15:39.570
but also the possibility

00:15:39.570 --> 00:15:41.760
that those mechanisms could be changed.

00:15:41.760 --> 00:15:44.190
Submission in the realm
of culture prepares us

00:15:44.190 --> 00:15:46.680
to be submissive in
the realm of politics."

00:15:46.680 --> 00:15:48.210
This submission is encapsulated

00:15:48.210 --> 00:15:50.640
by the passivity we experience
when binge watching,

00:15:50.640 --> 00:15:53.220
as we're directed to continue consuming.

00:15:53.220 --> 00:15:55.680
And arguably, when we
consume content consistent

00:15:55.680 --> 00:15:57.240
with dominant political realities,

00:15:57.240 --> 00:15:59.610
we're being primed to quietly accept them,

00:15:59.610 --> 00:16:01.740
or the opposite, i.e.
we're watching a show

00:16:01.740 --> 00:16:04.230
with relatively smart
social or political values

00:16:04.230 --> 00:16:06.360
but this only furthers our desires to sit

00:16:06.360 --> 00:16:08.280
in place and watch all 10 episodes.

00:16:08.280 --> 00:16:10.050
So either way, we aren't going outside.

00:16:10.050 --> 00:16:12.990
While art has long been seen
by artists and philosophers

00:16:12.990 --> 00:16:15.600
as a vital means of
questioning dominant values

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:18.930
or offering alternatives,
content tends to conform to them.

00:16:18.930 --> 00:16:21.720
That's true whether it's
glamorizing extreme wealth,

00:16:21.720 --> 00:16:24.060
emphasizing the social
importance of sex appeal,

00:16:24.060 --> 00:16:26.850
or reminding us that what
really matters is home ownership

00:16:26.850 --> 00:16:27.930
and the nuclear family.

00:16:27.930 --> 00:16:30.870
In this way, because of the
logic behind its production,

00:16:30.870 --> 00:16:34.080
it's very difficult for
content to be truly subversive.

00:16:34.080 --> 00:16:36.240
Unless it's us. We can, we figured it out.

00:16:36.240 --> 00:16:37.860
Upon reflection, it's surprising

00:16:37.860 --> 00:16:39.330
that streaming ever attempted

00:16:39.330 --> 00:16:41.550
to be anything other than an assembly line

00:16:41.550 --> 00:16:44.760
of capital C Content to
facilitate endless consumption.

00:16:44.760 --> 00:16:47.340
The early years, dominated
by prestige shows

00:16:47.340 --> 00:16:49.320
and indie films, simply
don't fit the logic

00:16:49.320 --> 00:16:50.790
of the way we consume content.

00:16:50.790 --> 00:16:53.520
The kinds of reality shows and docu-series

00:16:53.520 --> 00:16:55.050
and carbon copy rom-coms

00:16:55.050 --> 00:16:56.850
that have proliferated in recent years

00:16:56.850 --> 00:16:59.310
and to significant dismay
are far more fitting

00:16:59.310 --> 00:17:00.900
to the zoned out binging

00:17:00.900 --> 00:17:02.820
that the medium of streaming encourages.

00:17:02.820 --> 00:17:04.290
So, streaming isn't dead,

00:17:04.290 --> 00:17:07.500
but it's arguably a
zombified version of itself,

00:17:07.500 --> 00:17:09.240
stuck in a race to the bottom

00:17:09.240 --> 00:17:11.670
where no amount of content is ever enough

00:17:11.670 --> 00:17:13.950
and cheap utterly watchable entertainment

00:17:13.950 --> 00:17:16.290
that forgoes provocation is king.

00:17:16.290 --> 00:17:17.280
But what do you guys think?

00:17:17.280 --> 00:17:19.620
Has content destroyed
culture as we know it?

00:17:19.620 --> 00:17:21.900
And are streaming platforms
the main culprits?

00:17:21.900 --> 00:17:24.000
Let us know what you
think in the comments.

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:25.590
Thank you so much to our patrons

00:17:25.590 --> 00:17:28.470
for all your support, and do
check out our Patreon page

00:17:28.470 --> 00:17:29.700
if you haven't in a while.

00:17:29.700 --> 00:17:31.260
Be sure to like this video

00:17:31.260 --> 00:17:32.970
if you thought it was good content,

00:17:32.970 --> 00:17:35.640
and thanks as always for
hanging out and watching.

00:17:35.640 --> 00:17:36.552
We'll catch you later.

00:17:36.552 --> 00:17:39.135
(steady music)

